<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cieszyn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teschen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cieszyn (German: Teschen) is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a> river, which since 1920 has formed the border with the Czech town of Český Těšín. From 1290 it was the seat of the Duchy of Cieszyn, ruled by a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> until 1653 and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The division of Cieszyn Silesia between Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1920 split the town in two.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cieszyn.jpg" alt="Cieszyn" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SuperGlob · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cieszyn_001_-_Rynek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of Bohemia and Moravia, defended Slovakia against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with Poland over <b>Teschen</b>, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Maria Konopnicka, a poet and activist for Polish independence, wrote &quot;Rota&quot; (&quot;The Oath&quot;) in 1908 while staying in <b>Cieszyn</b>, as a protest against the German Empire's policy of forced Germanisation of the Poles in the lands Prussia had annexed in the partitions of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Bytom line died out in 1355, the duchy was divided between the dukes of Oleśnica and <b>Cieszyn</b> and later fragmented further, with parts passing among Silesian princes until the end of the 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bytom">Duchy of Bytom</a></li><li><blockquote>In the partition of Upper Silesia after 1281, Mieszko received <b>Cieszyn</b>, and by 1290 it was a separate duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cieszyn">Duchy of Cieszyn</a></li><li><blockquote>When Duke Władysław died in 1281, his four sons divided the duchy into the Duchies of Opole, Racibórz, Bytom and <b>Cieszyn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opole-and-raciborz">Duchy of Opole and Racibórz</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1315 it was split off for Wladyslaw, a son of the duke of <b>Cieszyn</b>, who became the first duke of Oswiecim.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-oswiecim">Duchy of Oswiecim</a></li></ul></section>
